000197147","module":"turquoise","kme":0.0646433,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.398013,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000197147","module":"blue","kme":0.124,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.680759,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000197147","module":"yellow","kme":0.207479,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.0240838,"module_significant":true}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000197147","entrez_id":["23507"],"uniprot":["Q6P9F7"],"ncbi_summary":"Contributes to volume-sensitive anion channel activity. Involved in anion transmembrane transport. Located in cytoplasm and plasma membrane. Is integral component of plasma membrane. Part of ion channel complex. [provided by Alliance of Genome Resources, Apr 2022]","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q6P9F7","text":"Non-essential component of the volume-regulated anion channel (VRAC, also named VSOAC channel), an anion channel required to maintain a constant cell volume in response to extracellular or intracellular osmotic changes (PubMed:24790029, PubMed:26824658, PubMed:28193731). The VRAC channel conducts iodide better than chloride and can also conduct organic osmolytes like taurine. Channel activity requires LRRC8A plus at least one other family member (LRRC8B, LRRC8C, LRRC8D or LRRC8E); channel characteristics depend on the precise subunit composition (PubMed:24790029, PubMed:26824658, PubMed:28193731)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}